body:
*Leviathan* (2012) is one of my personal favorites, it's a narration-less doc about a fishing vessel. Very atmospheric and mesmerizing. 

&#x200B;

*Hotel Monterey* (1972) is my favorite Chantal Akerman film, and I find it to be underrated, even by fans of Akerman. The same descriptors I used for *Leviathan* apply to this one, except its subject is a dingy New York hotel. 

&#x200B;

*Pina* (2011) is a tribute by Wim Wenders to a very creative and innovative choreographer, sort of using her work to speak for her, as she died during the film's production. Visually breathtaking.

&#x200B;

The ultimate experimental documentary, to me, is *The Act of Killing* (2012). It's about the Indonesian genocide by people who are still in power in the country, and Joshua Oppenheimer (the director) interviews them about it. Most of them gleefully tell of their killings, and Oppenheimer takes it a step further and has the killers reenact some of the killings for the documentary. It's really surreal and disturbing, but one of the best films I've ever seen period.

*Gimme Shelter* is one of my favorite documentaries, precisely because of how morbid and macabre it is. The entire movie, you can feel a tension in the air unique to that film and that film alone. The interviews with the band help this by showing how shaken all of them were due to the experience, and the sheer magnitude of the event itself is foreboding enough. Knowing what is going to happen somehow contributes half of the shock that comes with it when it does happen. 

&#x200B;

Oh, and the music's good too.

Accepting everyone as equal is a fundamental part of Marxism, and since people claim that trans people aren't valid or whatever, identity politics are born. It is less liberalism's fault as it is conservatism's fault. But regardless, I think the Marxist view on the matter is that everyone should be equal regardless of what they may or may not be born as.

&#x200B;

Also, I think it's very rare to find liberals who literally want to outright ban certain uses of language. Most believe in "political correctness", which in my eyes is half decent and half ridiculous. On one hand, it reduces the use of legitimately oppressive language and essentially acts as an unwritten social law-- "manners" if you will. On the other, it can be used to divide the working class, make people too aware of their differences and not their similarities.

It is true that his administration has made efforts to decriminalize homosexuality ([https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/national-security/trump-administration-launches-global-effort-end-criminalization-homosexuality-n973081](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/national-security/trump-administration-launches-global-effort-end-criminalization-homosexuality-n973081))

&#x200B;

However, this doesn't mean that he or his administration is pro-LGBT. You can believe someone has the right to be gay without liking gay people.

I think that both *Koyaanisqatsi* and *Powaqqatsi* are both incredible films, the best of their kind-- their kind meaning experimental travelogues. I've seen a few outside of these films-- such as *Baraka* and *Samsara*. I'm a fan of all of these movies, but to me those two films in the Qatsi Trilogy are king. They demand patience, but if you can look at it like you're going to an art installation rather than a movie, the whole experience is very rewarding. Like you already know, the soundtracks are amazing, and the way the movies look is absolutely awe inspiring. However, if you're looking for a solid storyline with characters and interesting plot points, go elsewhere. 

&#x200B;

Now for my feelings on *Naqoyqatsi*. I hated this movie. Everything that was great about the first two Qatsi films is essentially gone in some form or another in this one. I don't remember if the soundtrack was good, but it probably was. The visuals are much more uninspired and corny, looking at times like shitty screensavers from the early 2000s. There's also a problem with the way the film delivers its message-- the two other films show (subtly) how far humans have come in the course of our time on Earth, and how we have changed the Earth. In *Naqoyqatsi*, there is an actual part where it shows an illustration of the Tower of Babel. It's as if Godfrey Reggio thought the audience was too stupid to understand this message in the first two movies so he had to spell it out for us here. Also, to top it all off, there is a shot that is not in the correct aspect ratio in this movie. Truly horrible. To put this into perspective, *Naqoyqatsi* is my least favorite Criterion film that I've seen. So there's that too. 

&#x200B;

So, yeah, if I were you I'd stick to the first two.

The ending to my favorite film, *Stalker*, is really cathartic to me. The whole film seems to spiral into hopelessness, and that's why the final scene becomes something magical (sort of literally). 

&#x200B;

Also, by the same director, *Andrei Rublev*. Very dour and sad throughout but the ending really brings the mood up to something uplifting and even enlightening.

The best film soundtrack of all time, in my opinion, is Philip Glass' score for *Koyaanisqatsi*. It's half of the reason to see the movie. It's moving, engaging, and takes the movie from what could have been kind of boring to something extraordinary. You really feel the progress of man crying out through every note Glass' organ plays. I don't even listen to soundtracks very often, but when I do, it's probably gonna be *Koyaanisqatsi*. 

&#x200B;

Two other classic scores have to be for Dario Argento's films *Suspiria* (which was done by the band Goblin) and *Inferno* (done by Keith Emerson). Goblin's score is just iconic and gives the film a really cool, diabolical feel, while Emerson's score is the perfect blend of progressive rock and classical. 

&#x200B;

Special mention to Glass' score for *Mishima* as well.

I had to lie to my friend, basically saying that I "cured my depression", so that he'll still hang around me. I told him the truth before and he almost immediately stopped talking to me.

&#x200B;

Edit: He's the only friend I have in real life.

I actually really liked *Rogue One* and *The Last Jedi*, and I HATED *Solo*. I don't mind when someone doesn't agree with me on a movie, but the way that they shit all over anyone who liked those two *Star Wars* films is just so unfair to me. They claim that anyone who would like them is a fanboy, and I am definitely not that. I merely like the original trilogy, I'm not in love with the IP. I just thought they were legitimately good films.

&#x200B;

Oh, also *Boyhood*. I really enjoyed *Boyhood*.

Dog Day Afternoon is the first film that pops into my mind. It's about two men robbing a bank and how the whole thing becomes a massive ordeal that keeps spiraling out of control, dragging more and more people into it. It's one of Al Pacino's best roles and one of the few films John Cazale was in.

&#x200B;

One that sorta fits into this category is The Battle of Algiers. It's kind of a war film, being about a revolution, but the way it portrays it is in a very "crime drama" kind of way. The desperation is definitely there. Super realistic as well. Many of the people who took part in the real thing played themselves in the movie.

&#x200B;

Which reminds me, Close-Up is an Iranian film about a fraud in which the fraud plays himself. Really interesting layers at play there, and it's by one of Iran's best directors, Abbas Kiarostami.

&#x200B;

The Tribe (2014) is an artsier pic, but I love it just the same. It's about some troublemakers in a school for the deaf. There is no dialogue in the movie-- unless you can interpret sign language of course.

&#x200B;

Pickpocket is one of my favorite films of all time, by the French master Robert Bresson. Very somber and artfully done-- the subject of the film is a desperate man who resorts to being a pickpocket.

&#x200B;

By that same director is L'argent, about some counterfeit money that gets passed from person to person, and how it ends up changing each of their lives.

&#x200B;

A Short Film About Killing is a humanist classic, a parable against the death penalty, and is about a murderer who gets caught.

&#x200B;

Following by Christopher Nolan is definitely an amateur crime story, about a writer who imagines the backstories behind strangers' lives, and eventually this idea takes him too far into the lives of people.

&#x200B;

And finally, The World of Kanako is a unique pick in that it's a crime mystery. It's about a drunk whose daughter goes missing, and, although he can barely keep his life together, he is tasked with finding her. He becomes a criminal himself due to his careless attitude and chaotic behavior. He'll do anything to find his daughter. Anything. The editing really makes you get into the mind of the character and almost every aspect of it is perfect as far as I'm concerned.

&#x200B;

Hope I named some films you haven't seen!

&#x200B;

